- Requirement sets are cached per story file path, avoiding repeated parsing during a lint run.

4) Path validation and graceful error handling (Integration with file validation and error reporting, Error Handling acceptance criterion):
- src/rules/valid-req-reference.ts:
  """
  if (storyPath.includes("..") || path.isAbsolute(storyPath)) {
    context.report({
      node: comment as any,
      messageId: "invalidPath",
      data: { storyPath },
    });
    return;
  }
  const resolvedStoryPath = path.resolve(cwd, storyPath);
  if (!resolvedStoryPath.startsWith(cwd + path.sep) && resolvedStoryPath !== cwd) {
    context.report({
      node: comment as any,
      messageId: "invalidPath",
      data: { storyPath },
    });
    return;
  }
  if (!reqCache.has(resolvedStoryPath)) {
    try {
      const content = fs.readFileSync(resolvedStoryPath, "utf8");
      ...
    } catch {
      reqCache.set(resolvedStoryPath, new Set());
    }
  }
  """
- This:
  - Rejects path traversal and absolute paths via `invalidPath` diagnostics.
  - Catches file read errors without throwing, instead treating them as an empty requirement set, which then triggers a controlled `reqMissing` report if a requirement is referenced.

5) User-facing error messages (User Experience + REQ-DEEP-ERROR):
- src/rules/valid-req-reference.ts:
  """
  messages: {
    reqMissing: "Requirement '{{reqId}}' not found in '{{storyPath}}'",
    invalidPath: "Invalid story path '{{storyPath}}'",
  },
  """
- These messages are specific, include both requirement ID and story path, and are referenced by tests.

